Subscribe for updates from some external system, calling setState in a callback function when external state changes.\n\nCalling setState synchronously within an effect body causes cascading renders that can hurt performance, and is not recommended. (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect).\n\n/home/patrick/peachyhodl/src/components/ManageLinksDialog.tsx:39:7\n  37 |   useEffect(() => {\n  38 |     if (open) {\n> 39 |       setEntries(currentEntries.map(entry => ({ ...entry })));\n     |       ^^^^^^^^^^ Avoid calling setState() directly within an effect\n  40 |     }\n  41 |   }, [open, currentEntries]);\n  42 |","Error: Calling setState synchronously within an effect can trigger cascading renders\n\nEffects are intended to synchronize state between React and external systems such as manually updating the DOM, state management libraries, or other platform APIs. In general, the body of an effect should do one or both of the following:\n* Update external systems with the latest state from React.\n* Subscribe for updates from some external system, calling setState in a callback function when external state changes.\n\nCalling setState synchronously within an effect body causes cascading renders that can hurt performance, and is not recommended. (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect).\n\n/home/patrick/peachyhodl/src/components/ManageLinksDialog.tsx:290:7\n  288 |   useEffect(() => {\n  289 |     if (!entry.isEditing) {\n> 290 |       setTitle(entry.title);\n      |       ^^^^^^^^ Avoid calling setState() directly within an effect\n  291 |       setUrl(entry.url);\n  292 |       setDescription(entry.description || '');\n  293 |       setIcon(entry.icon || '');","react-hooks/error-boundaries","Error: Avoid constructing JSX within try/catch\n\nReact does not immediately render components when JSX is rendered, so any errors from this component will not be caught by the try/catch. To catch errors in rendering a given component, wrap that component in an error boundary. (https://react.dev/reference/react/Component#catching-rendering-errors-with-an-error-boundary).\n\n/home/patrick/peachyhodl/src/components/MarkdownContent.tsx:45:9\n  43 |       \n  44 |       return (\n> 45 |         <Link \n     |         ^^^^^^\n> 46 |           to={`/${nostrId}`}\n     | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n> 47 |           className=\"text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 dark:text-blue-400 dark:hover:text-blue-300 underline\"\n     | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n> 48 |           {...props}\n     | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n> 49 |         >\n     | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n> 50 |           {displayText}\n     | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n> 51 |         </Link>\n     | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ Avoid constructing JSX within try/catch\n  52 |       );\n  53 |     } catch {\n  54 |       // If decoding fails, treat as regular link","react-hooks/refs","Error: Cannot access refs during render\n\nReact refs are values that are not needed for rendering. Refs should only be accessed outside of render, such as in event handlers or effects. Accessing a ref value (the `current` property) during render can cause your component not to update as expected (https://react.dev/reference/react/useRef).\n\n/home/patrick/peachyhodl/src/components/NostrProvider.tsx:35:34\n  33 |   // mount, so reading refs inside its closures is safe.\n  34 |   // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/refs\n> 35 |   const [pool] = useState<NPool>(() => new NPool({\n     |                                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n> 36 |     open(url: string) {\n     | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n> 37 |       return new NRelay1(url, {\n     …\n     | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n> 93 |     eoseTimeout: 200,\n     | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n> 94 |   }));\n     | ^^^^^ Passing a ref to a function may read its value during render\n  95 |\n  96 |   // Derive the current signer from the active login. This mirrors the\n  97 |   // logic in useCurrentUser but avoids a circular dependency (useCurrentUser",["1103"],"react-refresh/only-export-components","Fast refresh only works when a file only exports components. Use a new file to share constants or functions between components.","namedExport","Error: Calling setState synchronously within an effect can trigger cascading renders\n\nEffects are intended to synchronize state between React and external systems such as manually updating the DOM, state management libraries, or other platform APIs. In general, the body of an effect should do one or both of the following:\n* Update external systems with the latest state from React.\n* Subscribe for updates from some external system, calling setState in a callback function when external state changes.\n\nCalling setState synchronously within an effect body causes cascading renders that can hurt performance, and is not recommended. (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect).\n\n/home/patrick/peachyhodl/src/components/ZapDialog.tsx:254:7\n  252 |   useEffect(() => {\n  253 |     if (target) {\n> 254 |       setComment('Happy Zapping!');\n      |       ^^^^^^^^^^ Avoid calling setState() directly within an effect\n  255 |     }\n  256 |   }, [target]);\n  257 |","Error: Calling setState synchronously within an effect can trigger cascading renders\n\nEffects are intended to synchronize state between React and external systems such as manually updating the DOM, state management libraries, or other platform APIs. In general, the body of an effect should do one or both of the following:\n* Update external systems with the latest state from React.\n* Subscribe for updates from some external system, calling setState in a callback function when external state changes.\n\nCalling setState synchronously within an effect body causes cascading renders that can hurt performance, and is not recommended. (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect).\n\n/home/patrick/peachyhodl/src/components/ZapDialog.tsx:323:7\n  321 |   useEffect(() => {\n  322 |     if (open) {\n> 323 |       setAmount(100);\n      |       ^^^^^^^^^ Avoid calling setState() directly within an effect\n  324 |       setInvoice(null);\n  325 |       setCopied(false);\n  326 |       setQrCodeUrl('');","react-hooks/purity","Error: Cannot call impure function during render\n\n`Date.now` is an impure function. Calling an impure function can produce unstable results that update unpredictably when the component happens to re-render. (https://react.dev/reference/rules/components-and-hooks-must-be-pure#components-and-hooks-must-be-idempotent).\n\n/home/patrick/peachyhodl/src/components/audio/AudioRoomBrowser.tsx:239:17\n  237 | function RoomCard({ room, onJoin }: RoomCardProps) {\n  238 |   const formatTimeAgo = (timestamp: number) => {\n> 239 |     const now = Date.now() / 1000;\n      |                 ^^^^^^^^^^ Cannot call impure function\n  240 |     const diff = now - timestamp;\n  241 |     \n  242 |     if (diff < 60) return 'Just now';","Error: Calling setState synchronously within an effect can trigger cascading renders\n\nEffects are intended to synchronize state between React and external systems such as manually updating the DOM, state management libraries, or other platform APIs. In general, the body of an effect should do one or both of the following:\n* Update external systems with the latest state from React.\n* Subscribe for updates from some external system, calling setState in a callback function when external state changes.\n\nCalling setState synchronously within an effect body causes cascading renders that can hurt performance, and is not recommended. (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect).\n\n/home/patrick/peachyhodl/src/components/auth/AuthDialog.tsx:138:7\n  136 |     if (!isOpen) {\n  137 |       /* eslint-disable react-hooks/set-state-in-effect */\n> 138 |       setStep('welcome');\n      |       ^^^^^^^ Avoid calling setState() directly within an effect\n  139 |       setNsec('');\n  140 |       setLoginInput('');\n  141 |       setShowKey(false);",["1104"],"Error: Cannot call impure function during render\n\n`Date.now` is an impure function. Calling an impure function can produce unstable results that update unpredictably when the component happens to re-render. (https://react.dev/reference/rules/components-and-hooks-must-be-pure#components-and-hooks-must-be-idempotent).\n\n/home/patrick/peachyhodl/src/components/livestream/LiveNextModal.tsx:38:17\n  36 |\n  37 |   const getTimeUntil = (timestamp: number) => {\n> 38 |     const now = Date.now() / 1000;\n     |                 ^^^^^^^^^^ Cannot call impure function\n  39 |     const diff = timestamp - now;\n  40 |     \n  41 |     if (diff <= 0) return \"Starting now!\";","Error: Calling setState synchronously within an effect can trigger cascading renders\n\nEffects are intended to synchronize state between React and external systems such as manually updating the DOM, state management libraries, or other platform APIs. In general, the body of an effect should do one or both of the following:\n* Update external systems with the latest state from React.\n* Subscribe for updates from some external system, calling setState in a callback function when external state changes.\n\nCalling setState synchronously within an effect body causes cascading renders that can hurt performance, and is not recommended. (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect).\n\n/home/patrick/peachyhodl/src/components/livestream/LiveStreamToolbar.tsx:37:7\n  35 |   useEffect(() => {\n  36 |     if (!liveEvent) {\n> 37 |       setDuration('0:00');\n     |       ^^^^^^^^^^^ Avoid calling setState() directly within an effect\n  38 |       return;\n  39 |     }\n  40 |","Error: Calling setState synchronously within an effect can trigger cascading renders\n\nEffects are intended to synchronize state between React and external systems such as manually updating the DOM, state management libraries, or other platform APIs. In general, the body of an 